7/s10711-023-00870-3)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; We define median quasimorphisms on groups acting on \\({{\\,\\mathrm{{CAT}}\\,}}(0)\\) cube complexes and their associated equivariant bounded cohomology classes.\n## 3 Median quasimorphisms\n\nWe define median quasimorphisms on groups acting on \\({{\\,\\mathrm{{CAT}}\\,}}(0)\\) cube complexes and their associated equivariant bounded cohomology classes. These classes do not have vanishing cup products in general (Example 3.19), but we show that they do under additional compatibility hypotheses.\n\n### 3.1 Preliminaries on \\({{\\,\\mathrm{{CAT}}\\,}}(0)\\) cube complexes\n\nWe recall the necessary basics on \\({{\\,\\mathrm{{CAT}}\\,}}(0)\\) cube complexes and median graphs [5, 54, 56]:\n\nA cube complex X is non-positively curved if the links of its vertices are flag complexes. Informally, this means that as soon as there is a corner of a possible cube of dimension at least 3 in X, there is an actual cube with this corner. We refer to this as the link condition. A \\({{\\,\\mathrm{{CAT}}\\,}}(0)\\) cube complex is a simply connected non-positively curved cube complex.\n\nThroughout this section, we will consider the following situation:\n\n### Setup 3.1\n\nFix a \\({{\\,\\mathrm{{CAT}}\\,}}(0)\\) cube complex X with vertex set V and an action of a group \\(\\Gamma \\) on X by combinatorial automorphisms. We denote the k-skeleton of X by X(k); in particular, \\(X(0) = V\\). The 1-skeleton X(1) is an undirected graph equipped with the \\(\\ell ^1\\)-metric D. Given \\(x, y \\in V\\), we denote by [x, y] the union of all combinatorial geodesics (represented by sequences of vertices) from x to y; such sets are called intervals.\n\nThe most important property of X(1) is that it is a median graph: for every triple of points \\(x, y, z \\in V\\), the intersection \\([x, y] \\cap [y, z] \\cap [x, z]\\) consists of a unique vertex, which we denote by m(x, y, z).\n\nFig. 1\n\nImage: Fig. 1\n\nFull size image\n\nHalfspaces in a tree and a square grid\n\nFor every edge e in X, there exists a closest-point projection, called the gate map \\(g_e :V \\rightarrow e(0)\\). We also write \\(\\{ \\alpha (e), \\omega (e) \\}\\) for e(0) and always make sure that the choice of labels for these two vertices does not matter. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAxes for median isometries | Annales mathématiques du Québec | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40316-026-00274-1)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last week; In some sense CAT(0) cube complexes correspond to discrete median metric spaces (see [4]).\nCeci généralise au contexte médian un résultat sur les isométries des complexes cubiques CAT(0).\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nA metric space is said to be median if any three points bound a unique “geodesic tripod” (see 2.3 for a precise definition). The class of median metric spaces is very rich with respect to subspaces and isometry groups. For example a Hilbert space embeds isometrically into a median metric space, and a finitely generated group has the Haagerup property (i.e. acts properly by isometries on a Hilbert space) if and only if it acts properly by isometries on some median space (see [3]).\n\nIn some sense CAT(0) cube complexes correspond to discrete median metric spaces (see [4]). In this paper we adapt standard notions and results for CAT(0) cube complexes to arbitrary median metric spaces. As a consequence we obtain geometric informations on certain types of isometries of median metric spaces.\n\nWe first review basic definitions and facts on median metric spaces. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nbig list - Counterexamples in universal algebra - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/154017/counterexamples-in-universal-algebra?noredirect=1)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; Median algebras are essentially the same thing as Cat(0) cube complexes so are fundamental to 3-manifold topology.\n7\n\n[Input]\n\nThis is only a counterexample in the loose sense of the word. Universal algebra students may hypothesize that all interesting fundamental operations studied in universal algebra and related areas have arity at most 2. Even though ternary terms are useful when investigating certain properties of congruence lattices (i.e. Mal'cev conditions), ternary terms rarely pop up as fundamental operations on algebras.. Therefore, algebras with interesting ternary operations are helpful to keep in mind. For example, median algebras are algebras with a single ternary operation $m$ that satisfies the identities: $$m(a,a,b)=a$$ $$m(a,b,c)=m(b,a,c)=m(b,c,a)$$ $$m(m(a,b,c),d,e)=m(a,m(b,d,e),m(c,d,e)).$$\n\nFor example, if $X$ is a distributive lattice, then $(X,m)$ is a median algebra where $$m(x,y,z)=(x\\wedge y)\\vee(x\\wedge z)\\vee(y\\wedge z)=(x\\vee y)\\wedge(x\\vee z)\\wedge(y\\vee z).$$\n\nSee this question for more ternary operations in universal algebra.\n\n[Button: Cite]\n\nImprove this answer\n\nFollow [Input]\n\nedited Apr 13, 2017 at 12:58\n\ncommunity wiki\n\n5 revsJoseph Van Name\n\n  * 1\n\nMedian algebras are essentially the same thing as Cat(0) cube complexes so are fundamental to 3-manifold topology.\n\n–  Benjamin Steinberg\n\nCommented Jan 9, 2014 at 14:56\n\n  * @BenjaminSteinberg That sounds interesting. Do you know a reference for the connection?\n\n–  arsmath\n\nCommented Feb 9, 2015 at 14:34\n\n  * @arsmath, look at personal.soton.ac.uk/gan/Roller.pdf\n\n–  Benjamin Steinberg\n\nCommented Feb 9, 2015 at 16:30\n\n  * @BenjaminSteinberg Thanks! That's fascinating reading.\n\n–  arsmath\n\nCommented Feb 10, 2015 at 21:15\n\nAdd a comment  | \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Isometric group actions with vanishi